 - 1 or h dollars, so 50(h - 1) ≤ 630 ≤ 50h. Thus 12.6 ≤ h ≤ 13.6, forcing the integer h = 13. Indeed, 20 days at $12 and 30 at $13 total $630.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p111","cnm_096"],"id":"daily-pay-bounds","label":"Bound the daily pay","methodDirectNodeIds":{"daily-pay-bounds":["cnm_100","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_100","cnm_099","cnm_p111","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 7 (N=16)","reason":"Bernardo's kth output is B_k=2^k(N+50)-100. For N=16, B_4=956, followed by Silvia's 1006, so Bernardo wins. For 0≤N≤15, B_4≤940 and B_5≥1500, so Silvia is last below 1000. Thus N=16 and its digit sum is 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"forward-bernardo-outputs","label":"Iterate Bernardo's outputs","nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_099"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (17 rods)","reason":"The two nondegenerate triangles require 4<d<10 and |15-x|<d<15+x. These intervals overlap exactly when |15-x|<10, or 5<x<25. There are 19 integer lengths from 6 through 24; removing the already-used lengths 7 and 15 leaves 17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadrilateral-side-bounds","label":"Bound the fourth side","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadrilateral-side-bounds":["math:quadrilateral-side-feasibility","cnm_h020","cnm_099","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadrilateral-side-feasibility","cnm_h020","cnm_099","cnm_111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-5)","reason":"The median always lies in [6,8], so equality implies 6≤(35+x)/5≤8, giving -5≤x≤5. Throughout that interval the median is 6. Therefore (35+x)/5=6 gives the unique solution x=-5, whose sum is -5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-the-mean-first","label":"Bound the mean first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-the-mean-first":["cnm_099","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_099","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-5)","reason":"The median must lie between 6 and 8. Equality with the mean gives 6≤(35+x)/5≤8, hence -5≤x≤5. In this range the median is 6, so (35+x)/5=6 yields x=-5. It is the only solution, so the requested sum is -5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-the-median-first","label":"Bound the median before solving","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-the-median-first":["cnm_099","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_099","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 16","reason":"The boundary vertices in order are (0,0), (2,0), (2,1), (-1,5), (-3,5), (-3,4). Its side lengths are 2, 1, 5, 2, 1, 5, because each slanted side has length √(3² + 4²) = 5. The perimeter is 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"eliminate-the-parameters","label":"Describe the region with inequalities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-the-parameters":["cnm_099","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_099","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. -2<x<3","reason":"The expression factors as (x-3)(x+2). The product is negative exactly between its distinct roots, so -2<x<3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_080","cnm_097"],"id":"factor-opposite-signs","label":"Make the two factors have opposite signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-opposite-signs":["cnm_h024","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_099","cnm_146","cnm_080","cnm_097"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-4)","reason":"On0<x<pi, sin(x)>0. Derive the sine-addition identity from the explicitly printed sine-difference formula using pi reductions, then obtain sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x) and sin(3x)=sin(x)*(4cos(x)^2-1), using the unit-circle coordinate identity sin^2+cos^2=1. Put t=cos(x). The cosine function is strictly decreasing from 1 to-1 on(0,pi): write cos(x)=sin(pi/2-x) and use the represented sine monotonicity on(-pi/2,pi/2). Thus each t in (-1,1) gives exactly one admissible x. Divide by positive sin(x) and factor a*(1+2t)=4t^2-1 as(2t+1)*(2t-1-a)=0. The root t=-1/2 is always admissible. The other root (a+1)/2 is admissible exactly when-3<a<1 and is different from-1/2 exactly when a!=-2. Hence more than one solution occurs for (-3,-2) union(-2,1), giving p+q+r=-4, A. Endpoint t=+/-1 and coincident roots are excluded explicitly.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"derive-identities-and-injective-cosine","label":"Derive the angle identities and count distinct cosine values","methodDirectNodeIds":{"derive-identities-and-injective-cosine":["cnm_h070","cnm_h064","cnm_h060","cnm_h173","cnm_h067","cnm_224","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h070","cnm_h064","cnm_h060","cnm_h173","cnm_h067","cnm_224","cnm_099"]},{"con